Anchoring - Represents the position of a parameter in a matched filename.  Each anchoring
	is represented by two numbers, representing the start and end position of the
	match.  Anchorings are represented as arrays of integers of length 2*N, where N is
	the number of parameters.  For instance, when "/usr/bin" is matched to
	"/$DIR/$NAME", the anchoring is given by the array [1 4 5 8].

Base - (a.k.a. base dir) The directory in which a rule is defined.  Determined by %cd
	directives.  When no %cd directives are present, the Stu process' working
	directory.  Represented as a string.  Empty when unchanged, and non-empty
	otherwise.

Command - A command that is run for a target.  Enclosed by braces ({}) in Stu files, and
	executed with the shell.

Content rule - A rule of the form "A = {xxx}".  Stu will write "xxx" into the file A,
	effectively hardcoding the content of the file into the Stu file.

Copy rule - A rule of the form "A = B;", which copies file B to file A.  Essentially the
	same as "A: B { cp -- B A ; }", but calls "cp" directly instead of using a
	subshell.

Depth - The depth of a dependency counts how many times over it includes a dynamic
	dependency.  For instance, an ordinary file target "file" has depth zero, a
	dynamic dependency "[file]" has depth one, a doubly dynamic dependency "[[file]]"
	has depth two.

Directive - A construct introduced with '%' in Stu source code.  Directives are processed
	before the source code is otherwise parsed.  They are comparable to C preprocessor
	directives, but do not implement a macro system.  Examples: %include %cd %set.

Domination - A rule dominates another rule for a given target if it has priority.  For
	instance, given the target "abc", a rule with target ab$x dominates a rule with
	target a$x.  For each target, there must be single rule that dominates all others,
	or no matching rule at all.  Domination is transitive.

Escape - Characters in a Stu script can be escaped by a baskslash.  For intance, \: stands
	for a filename that consists only of a colon.  (The colon is the target-dependency
	separator otherwise.)

File - An object in the file system.  We use the term in the broad sense, which includes
	directories and other special files.  The "normal" files which store a sequence of
	bytes are called regular files.

Flag - A flag is a syntax and semantic element of Stu scripts.  They start with a dash.
	An example is '-p' for persistent dependencies.  Can be written before targets or
	dependencies.  Some flags can be used only before targets (e.g. -P), and some only
	before dependencies (e.g. -t).

Job - A job is a child process started by Stu to build one or more files or phony targets.
	Jobs are run in the background by default, but the -i option changes that.  Jobs
	can be shell processes started by a {}-delimited command, but there are also
	special jobs, e.g. to copy files using /bin/cp.

Name - The name of an object, i.e., of a file or phony.  Names are strings of bytes, must
	have length at least one, and cannot contain the NUL (\0) character.

Object - A file or a phony.  Objects can be targets and/or dependencies.  Objects have a
	name that must be non-empty and non contain '\0'.  There are disjoint namespaces
	for files and phonies.  In a Stu script, any unquoted or unescaped text represents
	an object.

Option - An option is passed on the Stu command line and starts with a dash.  Some have
	arguments.

Parameter - A parameter in the name of a file or a phony target; written as $name or
	${name}.  In the command, the corresponding environment variable is set by Stu.

Phony target - One of the two basic datatypes ("targets") of Stu, the other being files.
	They are noted with the '@' symbol, e.g., '@all'.  A phony target can be built
	like a file, but will not result in a built file.  A phony target can also be used
	only for its dependency.

Place - The combination of filename, line number, and column number.  Used to print error
	messages.

Placed - A class name starts with 'Placed_' when it includes a place, e.g., 'Name'
	vs. 'Placed_Name'.

Plain - (Said of a dependency, target, or object) Either a file or a phony target.

Quote - Names can be quoted with single and double quotes.  They work largely like in the
	shell.  In single quotes, all characters stand for themselves except the single
	quote character.  In double quotes, the backslash, dollar and double quote are
	special (dollar can be used for parameters/environment variables, backslash for
	escaping).

Special rule - Special rules are a set if rules use dby Stu when matching parametrized
	targets to names of files and phony targets.  They are described in the manpage.
	For instance, a target ${dir}/file will match a target named "file" with ${dir}
	set to ".".

Target - The files and phonies that are built by a rule.  Written at the beginning of a
	rule.  May be followed by ':' to separate it from dependencies.

Trace - A line printed by the TRACE() macro.  Enabled by $STU_TRACE.  Only in the debug
	variant.

Transitive - A transitive target is a phony whose rule (a) contains no file targets, and
	(b) has no command.  Only transitive targets pass flags to their dependencies.
	Phony targets that have a command or whose rule also has file targets do not pass
	flags through to their dependencies.

Variant - Stu can be compiled in different variants, e.g. release, debug.  See
	doc/VARIANTS.
